  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 934 PM MST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nUpper low now over northeast Texas with trough hanging back across \nthe Big Bend. Moisture with the low is now well east of the area.\nDeep layer of moisture well south of our area persists, associated \nwith the sub-tropical jet. Northerly mid-level flow and westerly \nupper-level flow has ushered in much drier air (KEPZ PW on 00Z \nsounding down to .43 inches). High amplitude ridge continues \nbuilding in as the aforementioned trough dissipates ahead of the \nridge. Main Pacific upper low over southern Gulf of Alaska is the \ndriver of the building ridge as it digs southward over the next 48 \nhours. Thus through Thursday, expect mostly clear skies, light \nwinds, and seasonable temperatures.\n\nFriday through Sunday...Pacific low system moves to our area through \nthis period. Main Gulf of Alaska low moves onshore of the Pac NW, \nwhile a secondary low forms much further south, moving across the \nnorthern Baja Friday afternoon and then northern Mexico/far southern \nNew Mexico Friday night and Saturday morning, ejecting out ahead of \nthe main trough. Decent moisture contained with this low as PWs \napproach or slightly exceed .75 inches. Expect some showers to \ndevelop west of the RG Valley Friday afternoon and spread eastward \nFriday night with the help of the surface cold front. Good cold air \naloft (500mb temps cooling about 5 deg) will aid in supplying modest \namounts of CAPE and slightly negative LIs. So can't rule out an \nisolated thunderstorm with these showers. Low over Texas Panhandle \nby Saturday afternoon should end most showers, but the main trough \nfrom the Pac NW low quickly moves across the CWA Saturday night and \nSunday. Thus, the mountains could see a few more showers lingering \ninto Sunday morning. Snow levels over the mountains Saturday around \n9500 ft and falling to around 8500 ft overnight into Sunday morning. \nA couple inches of snow is possible at these elevations. Some breezy \nafternoon winds also possible Friday.\n\nMonday through Wednesday...shortwave ridge transits across the area \nMonday behind the exiting trough. Next Pacific trough comes through \nTuesday night and Wednesday. Main energy from this trough remains \nacross Colorado and northern New Mexico with surface front through \nthe area by Tuesday night. Chance of showers at this time limited to \nthe mountains with breezy winds Tuesday night and Wednesday. \nTemperatures remain below normal for this period.",
